<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wenzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wenzhou is a port city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the Ou River near the East China Sea. Its area was the centre of the Dong'ou kingdom of the Yue peoples in the 2nd century BCE, and the city became a trading port under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>. Since the 1980s it has been known for private enterprise and for its emigrant merchant communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wenzhou.jpg" alt="Wenzhou" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pascal3012 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale_de_Wenzhou.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wenzhou, formerly called Yongjia, lies at the lower reaches and estuary of the Ou River on the south-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, between the Zhejiang-Fujian Hills to the west and the East China Sea. The Ou, Feiyun and Ao rivers drain the area, and the terrain falls from mountains in the west to plains, tidal flats and offshore islands in the east. The Caowan Mountain site, excavated in 2002, shows human activity here some 5,000 years ago, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. A Neolithic cultural complex represented by the Haochuan culture gave rise to the Ou people, also called the Ouyue, part of the wider Yue people, whose capital was at Dong'ou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Ou had established the polity of Dong'ou by at least the fourth century BCE, and it surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> in 138 BCE. That year Yongning County was created, the first county-level administration in Wenzhou. Later names were Yongjia Commandery from 323 and Wenzhou Prefecture from 675. The walled city first established in the 4th century remained the seat of the prefecture and county until 1958. After the Tang takeover in the early 7th century Yongjia became a major trading port, with marine trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> beginning in 659. It was authorised as a foreign trade port in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Yuan periods and is regarded as one of the places of origin of Chinese celadon.</p><p>In 1367 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> took Wenzhou from an anti-Yuan revolt. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Pirates</a> called wokou were active off its coast, and the Ming court set up a tightly organised garrison system for coastal defence. Wenzhou became a treaty port under the Chefoo Convention of 1876, and a customs office opened the next year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steamship">Steamship</a> travel allowed local scholars to return with hospitals, schools and libraries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> set up a consulate on Jiangxin Islet in 1894, and in 1984 Wenzhou was named among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first 14 open coastal cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After 1978 Wenzhou was among the first places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to try market-oriented reforms, the Wenzhou Model, building on households with savings of over 10,000 yuan that had appeared in the 1970s. Since the 1990s large numbers of its people have migrated within China and abroad, forming communities such as Belleville in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Prato in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The city has the highest proportion of religious believers among Chinese cities, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> the predominant religion, and has more Christians than any other Chinese city, which has earned it the name China's Jerusalem.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wenzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 192 BCE Emperor Hui of Han made him King of Donghai, known from its capital as Dong'ou, near modern <b>Wenzhou</b> in southern Zhejiang.</blockquote><a href="/state/dongou">Dong'ou</a></li></ul></section>
